WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.420
Canada's new tariffs on U.S. goods raise the cost of everyday items for households on both sides of the border, while testing the next phase of trade talks. This is Omni View, episode 155. Canada announced tariffs worth $20 billion targeting U.S. milk, steel products, and plywood. The move follows earlier U.S. duties and is presented as a direct response to protect Canadian industries. Officials describe the list as calibrated to match the scale of American actions.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:43.040
The products chosen include staple dairy items and key construction materials that cross the border daily. Canadian government statements frame the tariffs as a necessary defense of domestic producers facing sudden U.S. barriers.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:57.900
U.S. trade officials have countered that the Canadian list escalates tensions without addressing underlying disputes over dairy access and metal imports. Both sides agree the measures will raise prices for importers and consumers in the short term.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:15.620
They differ on whether the tariffs will pressure negotiations toward a settlement or simply prolong the standoff. The BBC reports live coverage of the tariff announcement and immediate reactions from both capitals. The announcement adds a concrete data point to the ongoing trade track between the two countries.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:24.860
Trade representatives from both governments are scheduled to resume technical talks within the next two weeks, shifting now to a developing story in New York.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:34.100
New York State Assembly Member Zoraan Mamdani publicly opposed a planned event in the city that would host a leader from India's Hindu nationalist group.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:41.720
He cited concerns over the group's record on minority rights. Organizers have not yet confirmed whether the event will proceed.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:47.060
The statement came in the form of a public letter addressed to city officials and community groups.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:56.480
Mamdani, who represents a district with large South Asian populations, argues that platforming the group risks normalizing exclusionary politics.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:07.720
Supporters of the event, including some Indian American organizations, view the gathering as a legitimate cultural and political exchange that should not be blocked by one lawmaker's objections.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:19.060
Reuters details of Mamdani's statement and the event plans show the disagreement centers on where the line falls between free association and accountability for overseas political movements.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.240
Local media outlets have noted that similar events have taken place in other U.S. cities without comparable public pushback.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:38.280
Next, a shift in Israeli polling. Former General Gotti Eisenkot, a newcomer to electoral politics, is attracting voters who identify as both right wing and centrist in current polling.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:44.500
An interactive tracker shows his support holding study despite the absence of a clear ideological label.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:55.940
The tracker updates daily and breaks down voter coalitions by region and age group. Eisenkot's backers say his security background offers pragmatic leadership at a time of regional tension.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:05.000
Critics within established parties argue that his lack of firm positioning leaves voters uncertain about future policy directions on settlements and negotiations.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:14.580
The economists, interactive tracker and analysis of voter coalitions indicate that his appeal rests more on personal trust than on a detailed platform.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:21.680
Analysts following the tracker note that his support base includes both urban professionals and rural security voters.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.080
In Alaska, the Senate primary produced an unusual match-up.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:32.540
Dan J. Sullivan finished among the top candidates in Alaska's U.S. Senate primary held on August 18th.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:39.340
The race now moves to a ranked choice general election in which he will face the Republican incumbent who shares the same name.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.800
The primary results were certified by state election officials shortly after polls closed.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:49.740
Sullivan's campaign emphasized local economic issues and bipartisan cooperation.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.680
The incumbent's team has highlighted experience and committee influence in Washington.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:04:04.660
AP coverage of primary results and general election outlook shows both candidates must now navigate a ranked choice system that rewards broader second preference support.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.640
Campaign filing show both contenders raised comparable funds in the weeks leading up to the primary vote.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:21.800
Microsoft co-founder Bill Gates told rotors he hopes to meet Chinese President Xi Jinping later this year to discuss coordinated limits on dangerous AI model releases.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.640
He suggested China might accept restrictions if the United States moved first.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.900
The interview covered both safety concerns and the potential for joint technical standards.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:38.080
Gates frames the proposal as a practical step to reduce global safety risks from advanced systems.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.800
Chinese officials have not commented publicly on the idea.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:49.160
While some U.S. technology executives have questioned whether bilateral agreements can keep pace with rapid private sector development.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:57.360
Industry observers note that any agreement would likely require verification protocols similar to those used in earlier technology accords.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:05.100
An analysis finds that many British employees are neither fully working nor fully on holiday during traditional vacation periods,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.680
resulting in lower output than peers in France and Germany.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.720
The pattern is described as an epidemic of skyving and shirking.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.660
The study compared calendar data across the three countries for the same summer weeks.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.000
Employers cite blurred boundaries between remote work and time off as a productivity drag.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:29.240
Labor advocates point to long hours and limited leave as reason staff feel they cannot fully disconnect.

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:35.240
The data show measurable differences in average days away from desks compared with neighboring economies.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:41.720
Company surveys included in the analysis indicate that hybrid schedules contribute to the partial attendance trend.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:44.500
Now, some progress worth knowing about.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:50.780
Recent box office data show Gen Z and millennial viewers accounting for a rising share of theater tickets.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:54.240
Reversing earlier fears that streaming would empty cinemas.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.680
Films aimed at these groups have posted stronger openings than industry forecasts predicted.

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.740
The trend appears in multiple major markets tracked by theater chains.

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:09.820
Theater chains report that social and communal aspects of movie going are drawing younger crowds back.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:17.860
Streaming services continue to compete for the same viewers at home, yet the in-person trend has lifted revenue for several releases this summer.

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:24.540
The shift remains concentrated in urban markets and has not yet reached pre-pandemic overall attendance levels.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:30.340
Distributors have adjusted marketing campaigns to emphasize group outings and special screenings.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:38.280
Now, to really understand in this story, there is something most coverage leaves out about how governments coordinate on AI safety rules.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:47.340
The actual process involves aligning export controls, compute access, and model release thresholds across rival regulatory systems.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:59.100
When one country sets licensing requirements for advanced chips or training runs, companies must decide whether to comply globally or split their research, which raises costs and slows deployment.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:13.620
The last comparable effort, nuclear non-proliferation talks in the 1970s, showed that early bilateral signals can create templates later adopted by wider groups, but only when verification mechanisms are built in from the start.

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.880
In practice, governments often begin with shared technical definitions before moving to enforcement language.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:27.880
Historical records from arms control negotiations show that early agreement on measurement units reduced later disputes over compliance.

00:07:28.540 --> 00:07:35.540
Companies operating across borders face immediate decisions about where to locate new data centers once thresholds are announced.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:45.980
Knowing this, when you hear claims that a single meeting will solve AI risk, you can check whether the discussion includes concrete verification steps or remains at the level of principle.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:57.660
The detail worth watching next is whether any proposed limits name specific technical thresholds such as training compute measured in floating point operations that both sides would accept as binding.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:06.940
Readers can examine public export control lists published by the US Commerce Department and compare them with any joint statements that emerge from future meetings.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:12.520
If you want to go deeper on the tariffs, compare how Reuters and the economists are covering it.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:18.520
Tomorrow, watch for any signals from trade representatives as they prepare for the upcoming technical talks.

